+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+Request and application-context teardown each execute callbacks followed by a Blinker teardown signal. Both paths currently propagate the first exception immediately. `AppContext.pop` performs application teardown and context-variable reset in the same `finally` block, so teardown error handling must not bypass the reset.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+
+- Use a shared internal collector for the common callback-failure behavior in both teardown paths.
+- Dispatch teardown signal receivers individually so one failing receiver does not prevent other receivers from running.
+- Re-raise only after all cleanup work has been attempted, using the runtime exception-group capability when available.
+- Keep the existing callback ordering and asynchronous callback support.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+
+- Change ordinary signal dispatch semantics outside teardown signals.
+- Suppress, log, transform, or retry teardown failures.
+- Change the error passed into teardown callbacks.
+
+## Decisions
+
+### Collect failures in a private teardown runner
+
+Create a small private helper that invokes a sequence of teardown callables, records `BaseException` failures, and continues. Both request and application-context paths will use it for registered callbacks. A single finalizer will raise the collected errors: a base exception group on runtimes that provide it, otherwise the first failure. This keeps compatibility handling and re-raise policy in one place rather than duplicating nested `try` blocks.
+
+Alternative considered: wrap each public teardown method in one `try`/`except`. This cannot continue past an individual failing callback without duplicating dispatch loops and does not cover individual signal receivers.
+
+### Dispatch teardown signals receiver-by-receiver
+
+For teardown signals, iterate the signal's receiver iterator and invoke each receiver through Flask's async wrapper when necessary, using the same collector. Calling the normal signal `send` method is not sufficient because it stops at the first receiver exception.
+
+Alternative considered: catch around `send`. This would ensure the signal is reached, but would still skip receivers after the first failure.
+
+### Preserve context reset through existing finally structure
+
+Leave context-pop's `finally` structure intact. The teardown runner raises only after it completes its local callbacks and signal receivers; the enclosing `finally` then resets the context variable before the error reaches the caller.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Signal receiver iteration relies on the signal library's receiver iterator] → Limit the new behavior to teardown signals and cover multiple receiver failures in tests.
+- [Exception groups are unavailable on Python 3.10] → Detect the runtime capability and raise the first collected failure there, as permitted by D-17.
+- [Callbacks can raise `BaseException`] → Collect base exceptions so cleanup is still completed and use a base exception group when the runtime supports it.
+
+## Migration Plan
+
+The change is self-contained and needs no data migration. If rollback is required, revert the helper and restore the existing direct dispatch loops.
