+# Proposal
+
+## Why
+
+Teardown callbacks and teardown signal receivers currently stop subsequent cleanup when one raises. That can leave registered cleanup work unattempted and can prevent normal context-local restoration from being observed reliably by callers.
+
+## What Changes
+
+- Make request and application-context teardown attempt every registered callback and their respective teardown signals, even if earlier cleanup raises.
+- Raise teardown failures only after all cleanup has been attempted, preserving multiple failures as an exception group where the running Python supports it.
+- Preserve existing context cleanup semantics so context-local state is restored before a teardown failure is exposed.
+- Add regression coverage for callback, signal, error aggregation, and context-restoration behavior.
+
+## Capabilities
+
+### New Capabilities
+
+- `resilient-teardown`: Guarantees complete request and application-context cleanup in the presence of teardown failures.
+
+### Modified Capabilities
+
+<!-- None. -->
+
+## Impact
+
+Updates Flask's teardown execution in `src/flask/app.py` and exercises request/application context behavior in the existing test suite. Public teardown failure behavior changes from fail-fast to cleanup-complete, with grouped errors on supported Python versions.
